{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-223499","model_call":69,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ twitter \"twitter's growing pains\" (+ \"advertising revenue\" advertisers \"monthly active users\"))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ twitter \"twitter's growing pains\" (+ \"advertising revenue\" advertisers \"monthly active users\"))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_558386156#3_1321977782","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3168","rank":1,"snippet":", \"segment\": \"With the presidential election and Donald Trump tweeting multiple times a day, some expected a surge in Twitter's user count or a \\\"Trump bump,\\\" but it took until the first quarter on 2017 for it to materialize. Regardless, the company's revenue fell last quarter for the first time since its IPO, with advertising revenue plunging 11%. Much of the commentariat has rued the fact that such a big feature of the American cultural landscape is failing when it comes to the bottom line. <cover>Twitter's Growing Pains Twitter has a major user growth problem, a measure of an Internet business advertisers </cover>pay close attention to. Its monthly active users (MAUs) increased 20% year-over-year in Q4 2014, 6% in Q4 2015 and only 4% in Q4 2016. Its user growth bounced back to 6% in Q1 2017, a jump Twitter attributed to more interest in political news and technical changes on the site. However, as an analyst at Wedbush Securities said, \"One quarter isn’t a trend, so let’s see if it’s sustainable.\" Research firm eMarketer estimates Twitter's worldwide base will grow by 3.4% in 2017. ( See ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_558386156#3_1321977782","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":175},"model_calls":69,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":6,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
